INT. LIBRARY INSIDE A WHALE SKELETON - NIGHT

Ribs rise like cathedral arches over shelves of salt-warped books. Lanterns hang from vertebrae. A long table of driftwood. Somewhere in the dark, water drips onto stone.

OSRIC THISTLEDOWN, nineties, the Keeper, hunches over an open atlas, a quill behind each ear. Across the table sits MIRABEL ASHGROVE, forties, a courier in a salt-stained cloak, a sealed scroll tube beside her. At the end, fiddling with an abacus of bone beads, is PETRONELLA VOSS, twenties, the apprentice, sleepy-eyed.

OSRIC
You said the tide was safe.

MIRABEL
I said it was passable.

OSRIC
Those are different words.

MIRABEL
They're different tides.

PETRONELLA
Master Osric, the abacus is stuck again.

OSRIC
Hit it.

PETRONELLA
I did.

OSRIC
Harder.

MIRABEL
May I ask what you're guarding in here?

OSRIC
Everything.

MIRABEL
That's no answer.

OSRIC
It's the only answer. Every book in this room is the last of its kind. We keep them here because the sea took the rest.

MIRABEL
And the whale?

OSRIC
The whale was the first book. We just couldn't read it.

PETRONELLA
(quietly)
I think I can.

OSRIC
You think a lot of things, child.

MIRABEL
Let her speak.

OSRIC
She speaks. All day. It's the listening she lacks.

PETRONELLA
There are marks on the ribs, Master. Inside, near the spine. Like writing. I've traced them.

OSRIC
Marks are not writing.

PETRONELLA
These repeat.

OSRIC
Barnacles repeat.

PETRONELLA
In sixes?

A silence. Osric sets down his quill.

OSRIC
Show me.

She hurries to a rib and presses her lantern against the bone. The marks leap out in the light: tiny, regular, deliberate.

OSRIC (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Forty years I've sat under this.

MIRABEL
What does it say?

PETRONELLA
I don't know yet.

OSRIC
Then we shall have to learn.

A pause. Mirabel rises from her chair and looks up.

MIRABEL
I'll stay the night.

OSRIC
You'll stay the winter.

MIRABEL
I have a delivery.

OSRIC
Leave it here. Everything arrives, eventually.

The scroll tube sits on the table, forgotten.

At the doorway, the lantern light catches the wet footprints of someone who has just entered. A figure steps out of the dark: BRANWELL KESH, thirties, barefoot, soaked to the bone, carrying a dripping canvas sack and wearing a sea captain's coat three sizes too large.

BRANWELL
Sorry. The door was open.

OSRIC
There is no door.

BRANWELL
Then I'm very sorry.

He sets down the sack. A single book slides out, black with salt, and lands open on the table. Petronella gasps.

PETRONELLA
That's the same writing.

OSRIC
(softly)
Sit down, boy. Tell me where you found it.

Branwell sits. The lantern light finds the page.
